WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.180
A RAG system is a pipeline, not a single component.

2
00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:07.400
Source repositories feed connectors.

3
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:10.140
Connectors run ingestion jobs.

4
00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:16.640
Ingestion creates document records, chunks, embeddings, indexes, and metadata.

5
00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:31.140
At request time, an application receives user context, retrieves candidate content, assembles model context, generates an answer, and may write logs, traces, transcripts, or cache entries.

6
00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:33.420
Every stage has a trust boundary.

7
00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:36.960
A source repository may have its own permissions.

8
00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:40.100
An ingestion service may run as a privileged connector.

9
00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:44.440
A vector index may store derived representations of sensitive content.

10
00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:51.100
A retriever may filter by tenant, project, classification, or document-level access.

11
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:55.520
A model prompt may contain the final selected chunks.

12
00:00:55.620 --> 00:01:01.700
A user interface may expose citations, excerpts, exports, or summaries.

13
00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:05.660
Sensitive information can appear in unexpected places.

14
00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.920
Embeddings can represent sensitive meaning.

15
00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:11.300
Intermediate prompts can contain retrieved snippets.

16
00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:14.120
Debug logs can capture request context.

17
00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:17.420
Cached responses can preserve private answers.

18
00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:21.680
Exported transcripts can move content outside the original repository.

19
00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:29.060
Architecture reviews should trace data from source to final answer and then through logs, caches, backups, and administrative tools.

20
00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:32.300
Trust zones should be explicit before production.

21
00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:45.680
Teams should know which services are trusted to read source content, which identities they use, where data is transformed, which stores retain sensitive fragments, and which controls prevent a service identity from silently bypassing user permissions.

22
00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:50.580
Clear trust zones make later testing and incident response much easier.
